From 3e75f0727e1ab8bb077cdf2704f1e7291ed61329 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Rockefeller <alanrockefeller@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 11:16:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] CPU 4-state single precision: exact AVX2 kernels

AVX2 versions of the kernels MrBayes spends its time in when it runs
BEAGLE's CPU-4State-Single implementation: calcStatesStates,
calcStatesPartials, calcPartialsPartials and their FixedScaling variants,
the edge log-likelihood accumulation and state integration, and the
per-pattern maxima of rescalePartials.

Two patterns per 256-bit vector, one per 128-bit half, so all lane
movement is in-lane. Each value is the same IEEE single-precision
operation on the same operands, in the same order, as the scalar
expression it replaces:
  - sum_i = ((m_i0*p0 + m_i1*p1) + m_i2*p2) + m_i3*p3 is formed from
    matrix columns times in-lane broadcasts of p_j, added in that order;
  - no FMA: the functions are compiled for target("avx2") only;
  - sums that start from a zeroed buffer start from +0.0f;
  - the rescale maximum uses maxps(x, m), which is exactly
    "if (x > m) m = x" (NaN and equal zeros return m), and the maximum
    of that loop does not depend on visiting order.
The log() calls, scale-factor and double-precision site sums are the
original scalar code.

A tip state outside 0..4 (only a negative one, since setTipStates clamps
the top) is looked up exactly as the scalar code would. The kernels run
only when the CPU has AVX2; BEAGLE_DIKARYA_SCALAR=1 forces the original
code for A/B comparisons.

Verification: a harness that compiles the stock and patched function
bodies side by side (extracted from both source trees) compares every
output bit for bit on 90,000 random cases: 1-8 categories, odd and even
pattern counts, start/end sub-ranges, extra padding patterns, zero, -0,
subnormal, huge, negative, inf and (x86 default) NaN inputs, negative
tip states. MrBayes 3.2.9 runs with this build are byte-identical to
runs with the stock 4.0.1 library and with Ubuntu's libhmsbeagle 3.1.2.

Per call, 846 patterns x 4 categories, in cache: partials-partials
1.8x, states-partials 3.5x, states-states 2.1x, edge 1.5-2.2x.
