From ce198194e49e511edf9d6e837d4ed5dc0fc2642c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Rockefeller <alanrockefeller@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 21:29:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] CPU 4-state single precision: no subnormal assists; exact
 threading

Two changes to the same code paths.

1. Subnormal partials without microcode assists.

On x86 a single-precision multiply whose input or result is subnormal,
and a divide with a subnormal numerator, take a microcode assist (about
120 and 150 cycles on Broadwell); adds, max and compares never do. With
MrBayes's sparse scaler nodes, partials on larger trees reach the
subnormal range routinely: on a 129-taxon ITS job half of all partials
updates wrote subnormals, and the assists were about half of the kernel
time (measured by switching flush-to-zero on, which is not used: it
changes results).

Each category of each kernel is now first computed with flush-to-zero on
(denormals-are-zero off) and the underflow flag cleared. Flush-to-zero
alters a result only when that result is tiny, and every tiny result sets
the underflow flag, so a clear flag means the output is the IEEE result.
Otherwise the category is recomputed by a guarded kernel, under the
caller's MXCSR, that does every multiply or divide that could touch a
subnormal in double, (float)((double)a * b): a product of two floats is
exact in double and is rounded once, as the float multiply rounds it,
gradual underflow included; for division the double rounding is
innocuous since 53 >= 2*24 + 2. Conversions never assist; adds stay in
single precision in the original order. The tests that pick a path
(per-column limits from the transition matrix, an exponent-sum test for
products, tameness of scale factors and weights) only decide speed.

Each partials buffer records, per pattern chunk and rate category,
whether it may hold a subnormal, so a consumer whose inputs do skips the
fast attempt (it would assist on its inputs). The flags are a hint: a
wrong one costs time, never bits. With a non-default MXCSR (FTZ, DAZ or
directed rounding) the plain kernels run, as before.

2. beagleSetCPUThreadCount: split calls by pattern.

BEAGLE's own CPU threading sums site log likelihoods per partition, so its
result depends on the thread count, and it never engages on two cores.
For this implementation setCPUThreadCount now starts a small fork-join
pool (DikaryaThreads.h) when the instance has at least 768 patterns:
updatePartials runs every operation on contiguous pattern ranges, one per
thread (the caller always takes the first; a late worker's range is taken
over, never waited for), and the edge log likelihood computes per-pattern
values in parallel and then sums them in pattern order, as before. Each
pattern is computed by the same kernel code on any range, so the result is
the same for any thread count. Workers spin briefly between calls, then
sleep (BEAGLE_DIKARYA_SPIN_US).

Verification: the side-by-side kernel harness (stock vs patched bodies,
bit for bit) with values spread from 1e-45 to 1, adversarial inputs and
random subnormal hints, 120,000+ cases; a mutation test (one ulp added to
the exact multiply) is caught. Complete MrBayes runs are byte-identical to
the stock library at 1 and 2 threads, including a NaN abort at generation
~240,000 of a real job.

On a quiet host, MrBayes 3.2.9, one thread: a 129-taxon ITS job 27.8 s ->
23.3 s; a 36-taxon job unchanged (no subnormals, ~1% for the attempt).
Two threads: +35% on a 1191-pattern alignment, not enabled below 768.
